WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.042 --> 00:00:09.041
After Fantine died, the Thenardiers kept Cosette. It was not for love; it was because the girl was useful as a servant.

2
00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:11.456
The Thenardiers were dreadful people.

3
00:00:11.457 --> 00:00:18.271
At the sound of Madame Thenardier’s voice, everything trembled: windows and furniture as well as people.

4
00:00:18.272 --> 00:00:22.104
She swore like a sailor and could crack a nut with her fist.

5
00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.033
She adored her daughters, Eponine and Azelma, but ignored her son, Gavroche.

6
00:00:28.034 --> 00:00:33.779
Monsieur Thenardier was no better. He pretended to have been a sergeant in Napoleon’s army.

7
00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:39.175
He claimed to have risked his own life to save a wounded general at the Battle of Waterloo.

8
00:00:39.176 --> 00:00:44.473
He had even painted a flamboyant picture of his heroic deed on the sign in front of his inn.

9
00:00:44.474 --> 00:00:50.992
He dreamed of becoming rich, but at the moment he was fifteen hundred francs in debt.

10
00:00:50.993 --> 00:00:56.618
Young Cosette was the Thenardiers’ only servant. She washed, scrubbed, and swept.

11
00:00:56.619 --> 00:01:01.667
Although she was small and scrawny, she did all the heavy work.

12
00:01:01.668 --> 00:01:04.582
Madame Thenardier often beat Cosette.

13
00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:10.580
Monsieur Thenardier did not want to spend any money on Cosette, so she went without socks in the winter.

14
00:01:10.581 --> 00:01:17.310
The poor child was passive and silent. The Thenardiers’ inn was like a trap in which she had been caught.

15
00:01:17.311 --> 00:01:23.477
On Christmas night in 1823, Cosette was sitting in her usual spot under the kitchen table.

16
00:01:23.478 --> 00:01:30.595
Clothed in rags, she was knitting wool stockings for the Thenardier daughters, who could be heard laughing in the next room.

17
00:01:30.596 --> 00:01:33.796
Soon Madame Thenardier turned the faucet.

18
00:01:33.797 --> 00:01:40.963
Cosette held her breath. Drinking water was scarce in Montfermeil, the town where Cosette lived with the Thenardiers.

19
00:01:40.964 --> 00:01:44.167
It was a 15-minute walk to the spring in the woods.

20
00:01:44.168 --> 00:01:51.688
During the day a hired man brought water to the Thenardiers’ inn, but at night fetching water was Cosette’s job.

21
00:01:51.689 --> 00:01:59.776
Four unexpected guests had arrived earlier in the evening, and Cosette was worried that the water would run out before morning.

22
00:01:59.777 --> 00:02:08.354
Madame Thenardier frowned as she watched a thin stream run into her glass. "I guess there’s enough," she finally announced.

23
00:02:08.355 --> 00:02:15.187
Cosette exhaled and resumed her knitting, but a long time passed before her heart stopped pounding.

24
00:02:15.188 --> 00:02:21.687
Suddenly one of the guests came into the kitchen and said angrily, "You haven’t watered my horse!"

25
00:02:21.688 --> 00:02:25.671
"Of course we have," said Madame Thenardier.

26
00:02:25.672 --> 00:02:30.910
"I know he hasn’t had any water because he snorts when he needs a drink!"

27
00:02:30.911 --> 00:02:37.231
Cosette came out from under the table. "Oh, monsieur, your horse had a big drink," she lied.

28
00:02:37.232 --> 00:02:41.157
"I brought him the bucket myself, and I talked to him."

29
00:02:41.158 --> 00:02:46.947
"Here’s a girl the size of a mouse who can tell a lie as big as a house!" exclaimed the man.

30
00:02:46.948 --> 00:02:50.482
"Give my horse some water and say no more about it."

31
00:02:50.483 --> 00:02:53.736
Cosette slipped back underneath the table.

32
00:02:53.737 --> 00:03:00.966
"Cosette!" said Madame Thenardier. "Carry some water to this man’s horse."

33
00:03:00.967 --> 00:03:05.881
"But there isn’t any water," Cosette said quietly.

34
00:03:05.882 --> 00:03:12.299
"Then go fetch some!" Madame Thenardier’s voice rattled every pot in the kitchen.

35
00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.878
Cosette hung her head and fetched an empty bucket by the fireplace.

36
00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:21.140
The bucket was so large that she could have sat comfortably within it.

37
00:03:21.141 --> 00:03:29.267
"Here, Miss Toad." Madame Thenardier handed her a coin with the insult. "Get a loaf of bread at the bakery on your way home."

38
00:03:29.268 --> 00:03:32.252
Cosette put the coin in her apron and went out.

39
00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:38.608
There was a Christmas market in town, and a stall filled with toys stood across the road from the inn.

40
00:03:38.609 --> 00:03:46.442
In the place of honor sat a magnificent doll, nearly two feet tall and wearing a sparkling pink dress.

41
00:03:46.443 --> 00:03:48.701
Cosette stopped to admire it.

42
00:03:48.702 --> 00:03:52.346
"Oh, how beautiful!" She sighed. "That

43
00:03:52.347 --> 00:03:56.128
looks like the kind of doll a princess would own." "Stop

44
00:03:56.129 --> 00:04:01.775
your dawdling!" shouted Madame Thenardier from the inn’s doorway. Cosette

45
00:04:01.776 --> 00:04:09.362
ran as fast as she could with her bucket. When she passed the last Christmas stall, she found herself in darkness. She

46
00:04:09.363 --> 00:04:14.163
kept on going and hurried by a woman walking in the other direction. "Who

47
00:04:14.164 --> 00:04:24.322
is that child and where in the world can she be going at this hour?" The woman peered after Cosette. "Oh, it’s just the Lark." When

48
00:04:24.323 --> 00:04:31.068
Cosette came to the edge of town, she stopped. To go beyond the last Christmas stall had been difficult. To

49
00:04:31.069 --> 00:04:38.022
go farther than the last house seemed impossible. There was no light and Cosette was so afraid. She

50
00:04:38.023 --> 00:04:42.235
put the bucket on the ground and buried her face in her hands. When

51
00:04:42.236 --> 00:04:49.060
Cosette raised her head and looked around in the dark, she felt as if Madame Thenardier were there with her. Terrified,

52
00:04:49.061 --> 00:04:52.952
she ran into the woods until she reached the spring. She

53
00:04:52.953 --> 00:04:59.798
bent over the water and dipped in the bucket, never noticing the coin falling from her apron pocket. With

54
00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:06.862
great effort, Cosette set the bucket on the ground. It was so heavy that she could hardly lift it. She

55
00:05:06.863 --> 00:05:14.719
took a dozen steps and then stopped to rest. At this rate it would take her more than an hour to return to the inn. What

56
00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:21.517
a beating she would get! Suddenly a man appeared at her side and took the handle of the bucket from her. For

57
00:05:21.518 --> 00:05:25.291
some strange reason, Cosette was not afraid. This

58
00:05:25.292 --> 00:05:33.259
strong, white-haired man was Jean Valjean. He had not died when he fell from the mast of the Orion .

59
00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:38.166
In fact he had not fallen, but had jumped and hidden in a small boat.

60
00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:44.656
Eventually he had made his way to Paris, where he rented rooms in a remote part of the city.

61
00:05:44.657 --> 00:05:50.497
Earlier that day he had left Paris by stagecoach and arrived near Montfermeil.

62
00:05:50.498 --> 00:05:56.997
Valjean had disappeared into the woods, where he walked until he found a tree with a special mark on it.

63
00:05:56.998 --> 00:06:04.330
He had checked the ground nearby to be sure that it hadn’t been disturbed, and then resumed walking through the woods.

64
00:06:04.331 --> 00:06:09.060
At that point he had met the little girl struggling with the bucket.

65
00:06:09.061 --> 00:06:11.715
"Are you going far?" he asked her.

66
00:06:11.716 --> 00:06:15.227
"About a 15-minute walk," she replied.

67
00:06:15.228 --> 00:06:18.978
"Why are you out here all alone? Where’s your mother?"

68
00:06:18.979 --> 00:06:25.981
"I don’t think I have a mother or if I did, I don’t remember her. I live at the inn."

69
00:06:25.982 --> 00:06:27.873
"What’s your name, little girl?"

70
00:06:27.874 --> 00:06:29.005
"Cosette."

71
00:06:29.006 --> 00:06:34.044
Valjean had to get control of his emotions before he could ask any more questions.

72
00:06:34.045 --> 00:06:37.465
"And who has sent you into the woods at night?"

73
00:06:37.466 --> 00:06:41.703
"Madame Thenardier. She’s my mistress and she manages the inn."

74
00:06:41.704 --> 00:06:46.298
"Show me the way, Cosette. I’m going to stay at that inn tonight."

75
00:06:46.299 --> 00:06:50.166
Valjean walked quickly, but Cosette was able to keep up with him.

76
00:06:50.167 --> 00:06:57.378
By questioning the girl, he was able to learn that she worked all day while Eponine and Azelma played.

77
00:06:57.379 --> 00:07:02.426
"They have dolls and all kinds of things to amuse themselves with." Cosette sighed.

78
00:07:02.427 --> 00:07:08.284
"I don’t have much time to play, but when I do, they won’t let me touch their toys."

79
00:07:08.285 --> 00:07:12.746
They reached the town, where Cosette guided Valjean through the streets.

80
00:07:12.747 --> 00:07:19.846
She did not remember to stop at the bakery. As they drew near the inn, Cosette timidly patted his arm.

81
00:07:19.847 --> 00:07:27.169
"Please let me take the bucket now," she said. "If Madame Thenardier sees that someone carried it for me, she’ll beat me!"

